{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"tongariro","name":"Tongariro Power Scheme","operator":"Genesis Energy","note":"Western Diversion: the Whakapapa intake and a 16.5 km tunnel to Lake Te Whaiau, with intake dams on the Okupata, Taurewa, Tawhitikuri and Mangatepopo streams on the way; the Whanganui headwaters go by a short tunnel to the Te Whaiau Stream; Lake Otamangakau and the Wairehu Canal take it all to Lake Rotoaira. Eastern Diversion: Lake Moawhango (Moawhango River, 22 Whangaehu tributaries) to the Tongariro by a 19 km tunnel; Rangipo Dam diverts the Tongariro to Rangipo Power Station, whose 3 km tailrace tunnel returns it just above the Poutu Intake, which sends the river to Lake Rotoaira and Tokaanu (Lake Taupo). Not modelled: the Whangaehu-side aqueduct intakes, the Waihohonu intake (EA Report 4, 2.2: the Tongariro section begins at it), and the Taurewa and Tawhitikuri intakes (about 2 m3/s each). EA Report 4's Figure 2-3 (Genesis schematic, p. 4) shows the Taurewa and Tawhitikuri intakes on the Western Diversion between the Okupata and Mangatepopo intakes (from the west: Whakapapa, Okupata, Taurewa, Tawhitikuri, Mangatepopo), not to scale; no source found gives where they are (the Tawhitikuri Stream is named in REC2, up to 8.4 km2; no stream is named Taurewa in REC2 or the LINZ Topo50 river names), rf-6u2.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tongariro_Power_Scheme","https://teara.govt.nz/en/map/11834/tongariro-power-development","https://genesisenergy.co.nz/about/generation/rivers-lakes-and-rainfall","https://www.horizons.govt.nz/HRC/media/Media/One%20Plan/Jarrod-Bowler-for-Genesis-Power-Ltd.pdf?ext=.pdf","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf"]},{"id":"waikato","name":"Waikato hydro system","operator":"Mercury","note":"Lake Taupo control gates and eight dams. Aratiatia diverts the river past the Aratiatia Rapids (released a few times a day); Arapuni's station sits about 1 km below its dam, so the gorge between is bypassed. The others are reservoirs with the station at the dam.","src":["https://subsite.waikatoregion.govt.nz/assets/WRC/FreshwaterInfoSheetsHydroelecticity.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroelectric_power_in_New_Zealand"]}],"r":[[6134,2,1,97],[6135,2,1,97],[6182,2,1,97],[6183,2,1,97],[6184,2,1,97],[6185,2,1,97],[6186,2,1,97],[6187,2,1,97],[6189,2,1,97],[6190,2,1,97],[6191,2,1,97],[6192,2,1,97],[6193,2,1,97],[6194,2,1,97],[6195,2,1,97],[6196,2,1,97],[6197,2,1,97],[6198,2,1,97],[6199,2,1,97],[6200,2,1,97],[6201,2,1,97],[6202,2,1,97],[6203,2,1,97],[6204,2,1,97],[6215,2,1,97],[6216,2,1,97],[6217,2,1,97],[6222,2,1,97],[6223,2,1,97],[6224,2,1,97],[6225,2,1,97],[6256,2,1,97],[6257,2,1,97],[6258,2,1,97],[6259,2,1,97],[6265,2,1,97],[6266,2,1,97],[6267,2,1,97],[6268,2,1,97],[6269,2,1,97],[6273,2,1,97],[6274,2,1,97],[6275,2,1,97],[6308,2,1,98],[6309,2,1,98],[6310,2,1,98],[6311,2,1,98],[6312,2,1,98],[6313,2,1,98],[6331,2,1,97],[6332,2,1,97],[6333,2,1,97],[6334,2,1,97],[6335,2,1,97],[6336,2,1,97],[6337,2,1,97],[6338,2,1,97],[6351,2,1,98],[6353,2,1,98],[6354,2,1,98],[6355,2,1,98],[6372,2,1,98],[6373,2,1,98],[6374,2,1,98],[6375,2,1,98],[6376,2,1,98],[6377,2,1,98],[6378,2,1,98],[6379,2,1,98],[6380,2,1,98],[6381,2,1,98],[6386,2,1,98],[6401,2,1,98],[6403,2,1,98],[6408,2,1,98],[6409,2,1,98],[6410,2,1,98],[6411,2,1,98],[6438,2,1,98],[6439,2,1,98],[6440,2,1,98],[6441,2,1,98],[6444,2,1,97],[6454,2,1,97],[6464,2,1,97],[6465,2,1,97],[6466,2,1,97],[6467,2,1,9